<section aria-labelledby="enable"><h2 id="enable">Turn fragmentation on</h2>
<p>A frame has a size limit, 1,024 bytes by default, and some messages are larger. Fragmentation splits an oversized payload into fragments that each fit a frame, then rejoins them on the far side before routing, so handlers only ever see whole messages. It is off by default. <code>enable_fragmentation()</code> derives a configuration from the frame limit: messages up to sixteen frames&rsquo; worth (16&nbsp;KiB by default), partial messages evicted after 30 seconds, and caps on how much a peer can leave unfinished. Both ends must speak this format, and <code>WireframeClient</code> does not: a client talking to a fragmenting server sees <code>FRAG</code> payloads.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/large_messages.rs', 'budgets', title='large_messages.rs · explicit budgets') }}
{{ code.code('tests/large_messages.rs', 'enable', title='large_messages.rs · fragmentation on') }}
<p>The layering is fixed. Outbound, a reply is fragmented, then each fragment is serialized, wrapped by the codec, passed to <code>before_send</code>, and encoded, so each fragment is its own frame. Inbound, each frame is decoded and deserialized as an envelope, then fragments are reassembled and protocol messages assembled, and only then is the result routed. Each fragment carries a <code>FragmentHeader</code> (message ID, index, and a last-fragment flag) behind a <code>FRAG</code> marker.</p>
<p><code>fragmentation(Some(config))</code> sets limits explicitly; <code>FragmentationConfig::for_frame_budget</code> builds one from a frame size, a maximum message size, and a reassembly timeout. Remember that changing the codec or <code>buffer_capacity</code> clears it.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/large_messages.rs', 'caps', title='large_messages.rs · what the frame budget derives') }}
<p>A message over <code>max_message_size</code> counts as a deserialization failure. Exceeding <code>max_concurrent_partials</code> or <code>max_buffered_bytes</code> closes the connection immediately.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="fragmenter"><h2 id="fragmenter">Split and rejoin by hand</h2>
<p>The pieces the connection uses are public. <code>Fragmenter</code> slices a payload into capped fragments that share a message ID and count up from zero:</p>
{{ code.code('tests/large_messages.rs', 'fragmenter', title='large_messages.rs · fragmenting') }}
<p><code>Reassembler</code> is its mirror. It buffers fragments per message ID, rejects fragments out of order, enforces the maximum assembled size, and offers <code>purge_expired</code> for partial messages that never finish. It takes the two aggregate caps after its other arguments; <code>None</code> leaves one unbounded, as in the sample. The final fragment returns the whole message.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/large_messages.rs', 'reassembler', title='large_messages.rs · reassembling') }}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="assembly"><h2 id="assembly">Message assembly is different</h2>
<p>Fragmentation is wireframe&rsquo;s own transport mechanism, invisible to the protocol. Some protocols define their own multi-frame messages instead, with a first frame and continuations in their own header format. For those, a <code>MessageAssembler</code>, installed with <code>with_message_assembler</code>, parses each frame&rsquo;s header into a first frame or a continuation, keyed by a <code>MessageKey</code>.</p>
<p>Assembly runs after fragment reassembly and before dispatch. It can hold several messages open at once, interleaved on one connection, and, where the protocol numbers its continuations, it rejects gaps, duplicates, and frames after the last. A message that completes is routed as usual.</p>
<p>A declared <code>total_body_len</code> is a commitment. A body that does not match it is rejected, counted as a deserialization failure, and never reaches a handler, and <code>None</code> opts out. An exact total is also the cheapest way to refuse an oversized message early.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="budgets"><h2 id="budgets">Memory budgets</h2>
<p>Partial messages cost memory, and a peer can send first frames forever. <code>MemoryBudgets</code> caps three things per connection: the bytes buffered for one message, for the whole connection, and across messages in flight. Without explicit budgets, wireframe derives them from the frame limit: 16, 64, and 64 times it, which is 16, 64, and 64&nbsp;KiB by default.</p>
<div class="wf-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Memory budget protection tiers"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Tier</th><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Per frame, with a <code>MessageAssembler</code> only</td><td>A frame would push buffered bytes past a cap</td><td>The frame is rejected, that partial message is freed, and the failure counts as a deserialization failure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soft limit</td><td>Buffered bytes reach 80% of the smaller aggregate cap</td><td>The connection pauses briefly before reading the next frame, pushing back on the sender</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard cap</td><td>Buffered bytes exceed the smaller aggregate cap</td><td>The connection closes</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The soft limit and the hard cap count fragment and assembly bytes together, so a workload that fragments heavily can pace or close even with message assembly off. A default application buffers neither, and these tiers have nothing to do. The effective per-message limit is the smaller of the fragmentation maximum and the per-message budget. A message that completes in a single frame is never counted, because nothing is buffered.</p>
</section>
